{"id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:length-of-curve","title":"def:length-of-curve","path":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.md","line":2124,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex#def:length-of-curve","uses":["[[references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:riemannian-metric]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex","label":"def:length-of-curve","line":3431,"source":"\\begin{definition}[The Length of a Curve, extended form]\n\\label{def:length-of-curve}\n\n\\uses{def:riemannian-metric}\n\n\n\nFor a curve $\\gamma$ and $a\\le b$, define\n\\[\nL_g(\\gamma|_{[a,b]})=\\int_{[a,b]}|\\gamma'(t)|_g\\,dt\\in[0,\\infty]\n\\]\nusing the Lebesgue integral. For an admissible curve on a compact interval this is finite and equals the classical length.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[The Length of a Curve, extended form] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a6c656e6774682d6f662d6375727665\"></span> <p></p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FLeeRiemannian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2Fchapter2%23def%3Ariemannian-metric\">↗ def:riemannian-metric</a> <p></p>For a curve <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\gamma $\">$\\gamma $</span> and <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$a\\le b$\">$a\\le b$</span>, define <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"true\" data-tex=\"\\[  L_g(\\gamma |_{[a,b]})=\\int _{[a,b]}|\\gamma &#x27;(t)|_g\\, dt\\in [0,\\infty ]  \\]\">\\[  L_g(\\gamma |_{[a,b]})=\\int _{[a,b]}|\\gamma &#x27;(t)|_g\\, dt\\in [0,\\infty ]  \\]</span> using the Lebesgue integral. For an admissible curve on a compact interval this is finite and equals the classical length. ","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:coordinate-representations-divergence-laplacian","next":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:length-additive","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:riemannian-metric","title":"def:riemannian-metric","label":"def:riemannian-metric"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:length-additive","title":"prop:length-additive","label":"prop:length-additive"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:length-reparam-invariant","title":"prop:length-reparam-invariant","label":"prop:length-reparam-invariant"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:length-isometry-invariant","title":"prop:length-isometry-invariant","label":"prop:length-isometry-invariant"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:regular-curve-unit-speed-reparam","title":"prop:regular-curve-unit-speed-reparam","label":"prop:regular-curve-unit-speed-reparam"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:riemannian-distance","title":"def:riemannian-distance","label":"def:riemannian-distance"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:length-of-curve","title":"def:length-of-curve","tex":"\\begin{definition}[The Length of a Curve, extended form]\n\\label{def:length-of-curve}\n\n\\uses{def:riemannian-metric}\n\n\n\nFor a curve $\\gamma$ and $a\\le b$, define\n\\[\nL_g(\\gamma|_{[a,b]})=\\int_{[a,b]}|\\gamma'(t)|_g\\,dt\\in[0,\\infty]\n\\]\nusing the Lebesgue integral. For an admissible curve on a compact interval this is finite and equals the classical length.\n\\end{definition}","source":"tex/references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex"